modules.details_md:
`timeline(cap)` walks a ready `view.Capture`'s HTTP items for `HOST` (`x-csauth.ngs.nexon.com`; NGS is Nexon's anti-cheat). A *computer* is the request's `hwid` (the NGS machine id), named from its inventory report (`0xC210F070`) when the capture has one. Per computer (`Computer`) each event gets:

- `changes`: fields whose value differs from the last value that computer sent (`diff`), and `introduced`: fields sent for the first time;
- `reports`: `detected_info` / `collected_info` entries other than the `0x221FFFFF` decoys and the `0x00000000` heartbeat marker, which are only counted (`hidden`); an entry already in the previous heartbeat (but for its `log_id` / `log_timestamp`) is counted as `repeated`; known codes get a label (`LABELS`: drivers, process, USB device, VPN suspicion, ...);
- `response`: the reply's changed fields, and `config_changed` / `emergency_stop` / `detected` when set;
- `gap`: when a heartbeat comes more than `GAP_FACTOR` (2) periods after the last (60 s unless the server's `auth_cycle` said otherwise).

Left out as noise: the chained `csauthtoken`; `app_crc` / `module_crc`, which rotate through 5 answer sets the token picks (`crc32(token) % 5`), so each is compared only with the last heartbeat of the same set; `sequence` ticks (only ids joining or leaving, and a value an id never had, the latter marked `minor`). Events with nothing of note are `quiet` (the page folds runs of them). Bodies that aren't JSON are listed as `damaged`. x-csauth connections that couldn't be decrypted (no key) are counted per client address (`unkeyed`). The result is cached on the capture by `ui.tls._api`.
